    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Field observations reveal that the ratio of spacing to layer thickness ranges from less than 0.1 to greater than 10. The condition of fracture saturation explains values between 0.8 and 1.2, the most commonly observed spacing data. Fractures with greater spacing to layer thickness ratio are explained as the fracturing process having not reached the saturation layer. To explain smaller spacing to layer thickness ratios, we study the possibility for further fracture infilling, by considering flaw distributions between adjacent fractures. Results show that infilling fractures grow more easily from those located in the middle of the fractured layer. The propagation of a flaw located in the middle of the fractured layer is unstable, but for the flaw to propagate toward the interfaces, its height has to be greater than a critical size. This critical size decreases with increasing fracture spacing to layer thickness ratio. The propagation behavior of a flaw with one of its tips at the interface depends on the fracture spacing to layer thickness ratio. The propagation is unstable, when the fracture spacing to layer thickness ratio is greater than a critical value. When the fracture spacing to layer thickness ratio is less than this critical value, the propagation is first unstable, then stable, and then unstable again. An infilling fracture can cut through the fractured layer only if the fracture spacing to layer thickness is greater than another critical value. Otherwise, the infilling fracture can only partially cut the fractured layer. For models with the same elastic constants for the fractured layer and the neighboring layers, this critical value is 0.546. In terms of fluid flow through equally spaced fractures, there is an optimum spacing to layer thickness ratio, which gives the maximum volumetric flow rate. When the fractured layer and the neighboring layers have the same elastic constants, this optimum spacing to layer thickness ratio is 2.42.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
